{"bear_case":["Reported earnings are inflated by non-recurring items, so the 7x P/E is a mirage. FY2025 net income of $3.75B is about 1.39x operating income of $2.69B, which is roughly $23.9 per share on 157M shares. Management guides 2026 adjusted EPS to about $16.80, and about $14.00 of that is core. That implies a decline of about 30% from the FY2025 figure. On guided EPS the P/E is about 8.6x, and on core EPS it is about 10.3x. EV/EBITDA of 12.4x is not cheap.","About $2.80 of 2026 EPS (about 17%) comes from asset-sale gains. Those gains depend on a supply shortage that could ease if Boeing and Airbus production recovers. Management itself declined to guide to second-half gains. A 20% gain margin on sales is a cyclical-peak signal, and it is likely to mean-revert.","Free cash flow was negative in three straight years: -$0.97B, -$1.18B and -$0.68B. That totals about -$2.8B against about $9.0B of cumulative net income, so earnings are not turning into cash. H1 2026 shareholder returns of $1.4-1.5B, the new $1B buyback and the dividend increase to $0.40 are therefore funded by asset sales ($4-5B guided) and new debt, not by organic FCF. Buybacks of about $8B since 2023 have shrunk the equity base while debt stayed near $43B.","Leverage is thin on the equity side. Debt of $42.8B is about 1.9x the $22.6B market cap, and net debt is $41.1B. If aircraft and engine values fell 10% on about $60B+ of assets, the loss would be roughly a quarter of market cap. Book equity is only about $18.7B (derived from P/B and D/E), so a modest impairment would cut ROE sharply. The 18.7% ROE is itself inflated by gains and buybacks.","The earnings quality depends on Russia. Net income swung from -$0.73B (2022) to $3.14B (2023), $2.10B (2024) and $3.75B (2025). Insurance recoveries of about $2.5B against a $2.7B charge are a one-time reversal, not a recurring earnings stream. Appeals (Fidelis) are still possible, and this helps explain why net income is far above operating income. Investors capitalising 2025 earnings at 7x are capitalising non-recurring income.","The moat is narrow and cyclical. The advantages cited, such as scale, cost of funds at a 4.2% average, and OEM slots, are shared with SMBC, Avolon, ALC, DAE and BOC Aviation. Part of the current strength (lease yield +30bp, net spread +50bp, 85%+ extensions) comes from the industry-wide supply shortage, not AerCap-specific advantage. A 4.2% average cost of debt also reprices upward as old low-coupon debt matures, which compresses spreads.","Airline credit and geopolitics are exposed. Higher oil prices, Middle East and Asia-Pacific weakness, and airline defaults all feed straight into repossession costs, downtime and impairments. The documents give no disclosure on customer concentration, debt maturity ladder or covenants.","Key-person and execution risk: Aengus Kelly has been CEO since 2011, no succession plan was found, and the M&A and buyback story rests on him."],"assumption_challenges":[{"assumption":"A trailing P/E of about 7x means the stock is cheap","why_questionable":"The trailing EPS (about $20.4 per the P/E, or $23.9 from FY2025 net income) includes Russia recoveries and sale gains. 2026 guidance is $16.80 adjusted and about $14 core, so the normalised P/E is roughly 8.6-10x. A cyclical lessor at the top of the cycle usually trades at a low P/E for that reason."},{"assumption":"Negative FCF is just growth capex, not a problem","why_questionable":"The maintenance versus growth split is unverified. Negative FCF persisted for three years while capex ran at 1.1-1.2x operating cash flow. Cash returns are funded by asset sales and debt. If sale gains fade and sales slow, the company must either cut growth, cut buybacks or add leverage."},{"assumption":"Leverage of 2.05x, below the 2.5x target, leaves ample room","why_questionable":"The 2.05x is management's adjusted definition. The market-data D/E is 2.33, and total debt is 1.9x market cap. Headroom to the target is partly consumed by buybacks that shrink equity, and the definition probably nets cash and treats hybrids as equity."},{"assumption":"Supply shortage is durable and supports lease rates and gains","why_questionable":"It depends on OEM production and engine availability recovering slowly. Any normalisation would lower lease-rate momentum and the 20% gain-on-sale margin at the same time. Both earnings drivers are correlated with one macro variable."},{"assumption":"Strong management and buybacks create value","why_questionable":"Buybacks of about 40% of shares were done at lower prices. At 1.2x book and about 8.6x guided EPS, the marginal return on new buybacks is lower. Management argues buybacks beat sale-leasebacks, which means the company is shrinking its growth capital in favour of per-share metrics."},{"assumption":"A DCF-free valuation on P/B and ROE is adequate","why_questionable":"With no reliable DCF (the average FCF is negative), the valuation case rests on P/B and ROE. ROE of 18.7% is from a peak-gain year, so a P/B of 1.2x on normalised ROE (about 12-14% on core EPS of $14, which is about 11.7% of $119 book value per share) is much less compelling."}],"inconsistencies_found":["Trailing P/E of 7.05 implies EPS of about $20.4, while FY2025 net income divided by shares gives about $23.9 (P/E about 6.0). The two figures do not reconcile, probably because of share count or timing.","Forward P/E of 7.89 implies forward EPS of about $18.2. Management guidance is about $16.80 adjusted (about 8.6x). Forward EPS is also much lower than trailing EPS, so FY2025 earnings are not a run-rate. The thesis cannot use both the trailing 7x and a growth story.","The filings analysis flags net income above operating income as unexplained. The qualitative analysis cites Russia recoveries and gains on sale as drivers but never reconciles them to the numbers, so the quality of the $3.75B is unknown.","Leverage is quoted as 2.05x (management) and as 2.327 D/E in market data, without reconciling the two definitions. The filings analysis itself says the unit basis is unclear.","Management is rated 'strong' for capital allocation, while FCF has been negative for three years and the buybacks are paid for with sale proceeds and debt. 'Shareholder-friendly' is not the same as self-funded.","The moat evidence mixes periods: 99% utilization is a 2024 figure, an 85% extension rate is Q2 2026, and the financials are FY2025. The market data does not include H1 2026, so the valuation multiples and the qualitative claims are not on a consistent time basis.","The Russia recovery of about $2.5B is described as a success against a $2.7B pretax charge, yet the same sources say appeal risk and recovery timing are uncertain. The recovery is described as both mostly achieved and still pending.","The 'repurchased over 93M shares, about 40% of those outstanding' claim is hard to reconcile with about 157M shares now outstanding. The base to which 40% applies is not stated, so the claim cannot be verified from the documents.","The filings analysis found no business description, risk factors or analyst estimates, so most of the bullish qualitative claims (e.g. 16-17% cheaper narrowbody slots, extension rates) come from management commentary, not independent data."],"severity":"minor","severity_reason":"The balance sheet is large and leveraged, and FCF is negative, but the company is investment-grade funded, has liquidity of about $22B and has no sign of a solvency or fraud issue. The main problem is that the cheap-looking 7x P/E rests on one-off Russia recoveries and gains on sale. That is a valuation and cyclicality issue (normalised P/E closer to 9-10x, with peak-cycle earnings), not a reason to avoid the stock regardless of price."}
