 "bear_case": [
  "Earnings quality is the core problem. FY2025 net income was $3.75B, but operating income was only $2.69B, and about $1.8B or more of interest expense sits below the operating line. That means roughly $3B or more of non-operating income, such as asset-sale gains, Russia insurance recoveries and tax items. Neither the filings summary nor the qualitative work breaks this down. The 7x trailing P/E is applied to earnings that are partly one-off.",
  "Management's own guidance undercuts the cheap multiple. 2026 adjusted EPS is guided to about $16.80, and about $2.80 of that is asset-sale gains. That leaves core EPS near $14. At $143.85, the stock trades at about 8.6x guided EPS and about 10.3x core EPS, not 7x. Guided 2026 EPS is also well below the trailing EPS implied by the 7.05 P/E (about $20.4), so earnings are guided to fall roughly 18% or more from trailing.",
  "Free cash flow was negative in three straight years (-$0.97B, -$1.18B, -$0.68B), against about $9B of cumulative net income. Buybacks of about $8B plus dividends were therefore funded by debt and by selling aircraft. Per-share growth is partly financial engineering, with the share count down about 40% on a business with $42.8B of debt.",
  "Asset sales of $4-5B a year at roughly 20% gain margins are a peak-cycle harvest. They depend on a supply shortage (Boeing and Airbus delivery shortfalls, engine constraints) that inflates lease rates, extension rates and aircraft values. If OEM output normalizes, gain margins, lease yields and appraisals all fall together. That would hit EPS, the buyback funding source and book value at once.",
  "Leverage is heavy: $42.8B of debt, about 1.9x market cap, and $1.7B of cash. Operating income covers interest only about 1.5x. The 4.2% average cost of debt is a backward-looking blend, and refinancing at current spreads and rates would push it up. Lease yields reprice slowly, while debt and spreads reprice faster. The company's 2.05x leverage figure uses its own equity-credit methodology. The raw ratio in the data is 2.33x.",
  "Counterparty and geopolitical risk is concentrated. Airlines are weak credits and exposed to oil and war shocks, and the analysis itself notes weakness in the Middle East and Asia Pacific. Russia is a precedent for stranded assets. About $2.5B of the expected recoveries is not yet fully collected, appeals are possible, and the cash is lumpy and non-core.",
  "Key-person risk is high. Aengus Kelly has been CEO since 2011, and no succession plan was found. The M&A-driven moat (ILFC, GECAS) is not repeatable at this scale because there are no obvious large targets left. Organic growth then needs ever more capex in a market where FCF is already negative.",
  "The cheap P/E may be a cyclical-peak trap. Net income swung from -$0.73B in 2022 to +$3.1B, +$2.1B and +$3.75B. Earnings are volatile and event-driven, so a low multiple on peak earnings is not evidence of cheapness. The 12.4x EV/EBITDA looks expensive for a depreciating-asset lessor, because EBITDA ignores the real economic cost of aircraft depreciation."
 ],
 "assumption_challenges": [
  {
   "assumption": "Management is 'strong' on capital allocation because of heavy buybacks and a higher dividend.",
   "why_questionable": "Buybacks were funded by negative FCF, debt and asset sales. The 2023-24 purchases were made near or below book value. At 1.2x book and a record price, the same dollars buy less. A $1B authorization is small against $42.8B of debt. The dividend of $1.60 a year is only about 1.1% of the price. Gains on sale are described as unguided for H2, which shows management itself does not treat them as recurring."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Negative FCF is just growth capex and is therefore benign.",
   "why_questionable": "The maintenance versus growth split was never verified. Capex was $6.1-6.6B against operating cash flow of $5.4B for three years running. Lessors can show negative FCF while reinvesting at falling marginal returns. Asset-sale proceeds are not in FCF but are being used to fund buybacks, so the business is partly liquidating fleet to pay shareholders. That is not a self-funding model."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The moat is real because AerCap can buy slots 16-17% cheaper and has a funding advantage.",
   "why_questionable": "These are management claims from earnings calls and were not independently verified. The advantages are scale and cost-of-funds edges, not switching costs or pricing power. Competitors such as SMBC, Avolon and ALC are also investment-grade and large. The strong lease yields, net spread (+50bp) and 85% extension rate come from an industry-wide shortage, not from AerCap-specific strengths."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Low P/E (7.05 trailing, 7.89 forward) means the stock is undervalued.",
   "why_questionable": "The multiple rests on earnings that include gains on sale and Russia-related recoveries. The valuation module found no reliable DCF because FCF is negative. A P/B of 1.2x is not a margin of safety for a levered, cyclical asset-based business, because book value depends on appraisals made during a shortage."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The Russia recovery largely resolves the legacy loss ($2.5B recovered against a $2.7B charge).",
   "why_questionable": "The recovery is described as 'expected', not fully received. Appeals are still possible (Fidelis was considering one), and timing and collection risk remain. Recoveries inflate trailing earnings in the years they are booked and are not repeatable."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Lease roll-offs will keep lifting yields over the next five years.",
   "why_questionable": "That depends on the shortage lasting five years. If OEM output recovers, if recession or oil shocks cut airline demand, or if airline credit worsens, rolled-over leases may reprice flat or lower and residual values would weaken. The data also includes no maturity schedule or stress test."
  }
 ],
 "inconsistencies_found": [
  "Trailing P/E of 7.05 implies EPS of about $20.4. Forward P/E of 7.89 implies forward EPS of about $18.2. Management guides 2026 adjusted EPS to about $16.80, and core EPS is about $14. The data-provider forward EPS is therefore stale or too high relative to guidance, which flatters the forward multiple.",
  "FY2025 net income divided by the current share count gives about $23.9 per share, versus about $20.4 implied by the P/E. The gap suggests the TTM figures differ from FY2025 or reflect a different share base. The filings analysis noted this but treated it as minor. It also means the FY2025 financials in the history and the TTM multiples are on different bases.",
  "The qualitative section rates management 'strong' while also noting earnings depend on cyclical gains on sale, that Russia recoveries are unresolved, and that FCF has been negative for three years. The rating and the evidence pull in opposite directions.",
  "P/B of 1.207 divided by P/E of 7.05 implies ROE of about 17.1%. The data shows 18.66%, and management cites adjusted ROE of 18%. These are three different bases, and none is reconciled to GAAP.",
  "Leverage appears as 2.33x (data debt-to-equity) and 2.05x (management's definition). The qualitative section cites 'below the 2.5x target' without noting that the raw ratio is about 0.3x higher and that management's number uses equity credit.",
  "Net income exceeds operating income by 1.4-1.5x, which is unusual for a company with about $1.8B or more of annual interest cost. The filings analysis flags this but never reconciles it, so the real earnings power is unknown. The moat and management sections then lean on EPS and ROE figures that depend on those same items.",
  "The moat section cites 99% utilization from 2024 next to Q2 2026 lease metrics, mixing periods. The 16-17% slot-price advantage and other claims rest on call transcripts and aggregator summaries, not on filings.",
  "The filings analysis states that no filings or risk factors were provided. The qualitative section nonetheless cites 10-K/20-F and 2026 documents. Quantitative history (to FY2025) and qualitative evidence (to Q3 2026) were not reconciled, and the 2026 YTD financials were not used."
 ],
 "severity": "minor",
 "severity_reason": "The issues are real: negative FCF for three years, high leverage, earnings inflated by gains and one-off recoveries, and guided 2026 EPS well below trailing. They are valuation and earnings-quality concerns, not evidence of fraud or solvency risk. The company is investment-grade, has about $22B of liquidity, and shows leverage inside its target on its own definition. The company reports the gains and Russia recoveries, so the thesis can be repriced for them rather than avoided outright. Reasons to avoid would be an unexplained collapse of the supply shortage, a funding-market shock, or airline credit deterioration. None of those is evident in the data, but the cheap-looking multiple overstates the margin of safety."
}
